  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<p><em>Neolamprologus lemairii</em> is a piscivorous cichlid endemic to Lake Tanganyika, growing to around 25 cm. An ambush predator, it lies motionless on the substrate so that its mottled pattern makes it appear dead to the smaller fish that come to investigate. It is unsuited to the general aquarium and should be kept alone, with tankmates too large to be considered food. In nature it spawns in caves.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<p>Endemic to Lake Tanganyika (Burundi, DR Congo, Tanzania and Zambia).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<p>This species has no specific habitat, although it is most often seen around the shorelines of the lake.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<p>Standard length: 25 cm SL. Aquarium base: 120 × 45 cm. Volume: ~205 litres (120 × 45 × 38 cm).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<p><em>N. lemairii</em> has no special requirements with regards to decor. A sandy substrate is preferable, and the tank can be decorated with piles of rocks as with general Tanganyikan setups.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 23–27°C</li><li>pH: 7.5–9.0</li><li>Hardness: 10–25 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<p>It is an exclusive piscivore in nature but there is no need to feed live fish in aquaria, as it accepts dead foods readily. Feed a meaty diet of prawns, sand lance, whitebait etc. Do not feed animal meat of any kind.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<p>Should only be kept with robust species too large to be considered food. Suitable tankmates include Frontosa, <em>Benthochromis tricoti</em> and other large Rift Lake species. It is a loner in nature and therefore should be kept as a solitary specimen in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<p>Cannot be sexed by external means.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<p>It has not been bred in captivity but is known to spawn in caves in nature.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/neolamprologus-lemairii",
      "html": "<p>A highly piscivorous, predatory species unsuited to the general aquarium. It is an ambush predator in nature, often being seen hanging motionless around rocky structures or on the floor of the lake. It is a sedentary species and does not move around a lot. This is a behavioural adaptation known as thanatosis, or 'playing dead'. This strategy is usually used as a defensive means in other animals, but in <em>N. lemairii</em> it is adapted for offensive means. The fish has a mottled colour pattern and this, combined with the aforementioned ability to lie motionless on the substrate, makes the fish appear dead to unsuspecting prey. When a smaller fish attempts to investigate the 'corpse' it receives a nasty surprise! This fish is not recommended for the home aquarium, except to true enthusiasts.</p>"
    }
  ],
